#import "/_lib/0.1.1.typ": *
#show: template

#taxon[Lemma]
#title[Identity type of Cartesian Decompositions]

Fix a finite set $S$. Let $(A,B,e),(A',B',e') : "cart-dec"(S)$.
There is an equivalence
$
((A,B,e) = (A',B',e')) ~= sum_(f: A ~= A') sum_(g : B ~= B') e' @ (f xx g) ~ e,
$
where $(f xx g ) : (A xx B) ->  (A' xx B')$ is the induced map from the equivalences $f$ and $g$.
We denote the rhs of the equivalence with $"Eq-cart-dec"(S)((A,B,e), (A',B',e'))$

#proof[

  Define $C(A',B',e')$ to be the rhs of the equivalence we want to show.

  Notice that
  $
  C(A, B, e) &-= sum_(f: A ~= A) sum_(g : B ~= B) e @ (f xx g) ~ e\
  &~= sum_(f: A ~= A) sum_(g : B ~= B) f xx g ~ "id"
  $

  Let $f-="id"_A$, $g -= "id"_B$, then for any $(a,b) : A xx B$, $f(a,b) -= (a,b)$. In other words, we get a homotopy $alpha : f xx g ~ "id"$. Thus
  $(f,g,alpha) : C(A,B,e)$.
  Furthermore $B$ is torsorial since
  $
    sum_((A',B',e') : "cart-dec"(S)) C(A',B',e') &-= sum_(A' ,B' : "FinSet") sum_(e': A' xx B' ~= S) sum_(f: A ~= A') sum_(g : B ~= B') e' @ (f xx g) ~ e\
    "(reassociate)" .. &~= sum_(A' : "FinSet") sum_(f: A ~= A') sum_(B' : "FinSet") sum_(g : B ~= B') sum_(e': A' xx B' ~= S) e' @ (f xx g) ~ e\
    "(univalence)" .. &~= sum_(A' : "FinSet") sum_(p: A = A') sum_(B' : "FinSet") sum_(q : B = B') sum_(e': A' xx B' ~= S) e' @ (tilde(p) xx tilde(q)) ~ e\
    "(contract)" .. &~= sum_(B' : "FinSet") sum_(q : B = B') sum_(e': A xx B' ~= S) e' @ ("id"_A xx tilde(q)) ~ e\
    "(contract)" .. &~= sum_(e': A xx B ~= S) e' @ ("id"_A xx "id"_B) ~ e\
    &~= sum_(e': A xx B ~= S) e' ~ e\
    "(function extensionality)" .. &~= sum_(e': A xx B ~= S) e' = e\
    "(contract)" .. &~= bb(1).
  $

  Applying the #local("00EK")[Fundamental Theorem of Identity Types] completes the proof.
]

// An elemary proof

// #proof[
//   Denote by $C$ the type family $C(X,Y) :-= X xx_"FinSet" Y ~= S$.
//   $
//     ((A,B,e) =_"cart-dec"(S) (A',B',e')) &~= (sum (p: A ~= A', q : B ~= B'), "tr"_C ("pair"(p,q), e) = e')\
//     &~=_J (sum (p: A ~= A', q : B ~= B'), e' @ (p xx q) =_C(A,B) e).
//   $
//
//   In the second step, we make use of the $J$ rule:
//
//   For a path $r: (A,B) -> (A',B')$ denote by $overline(r)$ the induced path
//   $overline(r) : A xx B -> A' xx B'$. then for $e : C(A,B)$ and $e' : C(A',B')$
//   we get a term of type
//   $
//     ("tr"_C (r,e) = e') ~= (e' @ overline(r) = e),
//   $
//   by doing path induction on $r$: When $r -= "refl"_(A,B)$, we get
//   $
//     "tr"_C ("refl",e) -= e, .. overline("refl") = "id"_(A xx B).
//   $
//   So it suffices to provide a term of type $(e = e') ~= (e' @ id_(A xx B) = e)$, which is (up to unit-path) given by reversal of paths.
// ]
